**Theorem 10.6 (The exact future quotient for base-only outputs).**
The declared task observes only the four bottom native edges and the
lawfulness of each relative command, including the empty command. Two
states in $\mathcal M=(0,L)\times\mathbb T^2$ have the same full behavior
if and only if their $(\kappa,e^{i(u-v)})$ coordinates agree. Thus the
behavioral quotient has exactly the topology and smooth structure (10.9).
It has a smooth global section, while the common phase remains invisible.

Proof. For a lawful command the single independent bottom coherence is

$$
z_\gamma=\sqrt{(\kappa+A)(L-\kappa-A)}
 e^{i(u-v)+iB/2}.
\tag{10.13}
$$

Its other bottom outputs are its copy and conjugates, followed by their
fixed preparation rotations. The lawful domain (10.10) depends only on
$\kappa$. Hence equality of $(\kappa,e^{i(u-v)})$ gives equality of all
outputs and refusal symbols. In particular a common increment
$(u,v)\mapsto(u+\lambda,v+\lambda)$ stays invisible after every command;
(10.11) adds the same height-dependent common term to both candidates.

Conversely the empty command fixes $e^{i(u-v)}$ and the positive magnitude
$\sqrt{\kappa(L-\kappa)}$, leaving only the possible reflection
$\kappa\leftrightarrow L-\kappa$. For any proposed pair of interior
heights choose a sufficiently small nonzero $A$ so that the straight
height command from zero to $A$ is lawful at both. This is a common
command for that pair, not a claim of lawfulness on the whole fiber.
Its two observations satisfy

$$
|z_A|^2-|z_0|^2=A(L-2\kappa)-A^2,
\qquad
\kappa=\frac{L-A-(|z_A|^2-|z_0|^2)/A}{2}.
\tag{10.14}
$$

The identity follows by expanding
$(\kappa+A)(L-\kappa-A)-\kappa(L-\kappa)$.
Therefore identical behaviors force the same height. The case
$\kappa=L/2$ already has only one reflected candidate.
The circle coordinate then agrees by the empty output, proving necessity.
Proposition 10.4 establishes the actual quotient topology and section.
If the $02$ output is added to this task, its empty-command values already
recover the full current state by (10.8), in agreement with Transport
Theorem 9.7. $\square$

